cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use Closed Source: The God that Failed, please cite it using the metadata below."
type: software
title: "Closed Source: The God that Failed"
abstract: >-
  A position paper arguing that closed source has failed as a protection, for science first. Large
  language models now decompile binaries into re-executable source; black-box queries extract
  parts of production models; distillation copies capabilities at industrial scale; and the
  largest 2026 trade-secret dispute in artificial intelligence, Apple's suit against OpenAI, turns
  on people, laptops and recruiting rather than on code that stayed secret.

  Against this background the paper revisits Linus Torvalds' Linux and Git as evidence that
  openness scales, and the author's closed undergraduate thesis as evidence of what secrecy costs
  a researcher. It argues that the ethical answer to universal reverse engineering is professional
  citation, proposes three rules for AI agents (borrow, cite, propagate), and describes Paper
  Fabric, the open pipeline in the Ouroboric style that produced it.

  This record is a book. It has not been peer reviewed. It holds the source of the book at the
  release tag: the Markdown text, the build and the agent configuration. The rendered PDFs are
  attached to the GitHub release of the same tag.

  Preview the full text as a PDF in the browser:
  https://h0ffmann.github.io/closed-source/paper.pdf.

  The writer develops in the open. His three other works each have a permanent DOI, and this
  record cites all three: marola, an open, non-profit platform for sea conditions and
  bathing-water quality, written with five other people (10.5281/zenodo.23224155); WW3 GPU Lab, a
  bit-for-bit GPU port of WAVEWATCH III (10.5281/zenodo.23221351); and picasso, Nix flakes for
  scientific and agentic toolchains, which builds this book (10.5281/zenodo.23311208). The book is
  made in the Ouroboric style, and the Ouroboric ideas are Bruno Valério's
  (https://github.com/ouroboric-dev/ouroboric); Ouroboric has no DOI yet, so this record cites it
  by its address.
